        <div id="sun-hours" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Sun-Hours Math: What a Panel Really Delivers
          </h2>

          <p>
            A panel's rated wattage is measured under standard test conditions: strong, perpendicular light on a cool cell. Real panels in a real yard almost never see that. Heat, dust, imperfect aim, wiring losses and the charge controller all take a share, and portable panels typically deliver about 60-75 percent of their rating at the best part of the day. A 200W folding panel that reads 130 to 150W on the station's display at noon is behaving normally, not failing, and planning around the label number is the most common reason a system disappoints.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second number is peak sun hours: the day's sunlight expressed as the equivalent number of hours at full strength. A clear summer day across much of the U.S. gives four to five; winter in northern states can fall to two or three; the desert Southwest in summer runs higher. Peak sun hours are not the same as daylight hours. A fourteen-hour June day in Ohio still only adds up to roughly five hours of full-strength sun, because the morning and evening light arrives at a low angle and carries far less energy.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE HARVEST RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Daily harvest is roughly panel watts x 0.6-0.75 x peak sun hours.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">At four to five sun hours, that works out to roughly 2.5-3.5Wh per rated panel watt per day. A 200W panel gives about 500-700Wh; 400W gives about 1.0-1.4kWh; 1,600W of rigid panels gives about 4-5.6kWh.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
