          <p>
            Notice the left-hand column. A heavily overcast or short winter day can cut harvest in half or worse, and a storm that knocks the power out is rarely a good solar day. That is why every array in this guide is sized with a margin, and why every serious solar generator plan keeps wall charging, car charging or a fuel-burning <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">home generator for outages</a> as the fallback for a run of grey days. Solar is a renewable fuel, but it arrives on its own schedule.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can look up your own sun hours rather than guessing. Free tools such as NREL's PVWatts calculator give month-by-month figures for any U.S. address, and the number for your worst outage month is the one to size around. If your power usually fails in winter ice storms, a summer figure of five hours will flatter the plan badly; if it fails in summer thunderstorms and hurricanes, you can afford to be more optimistic. Either way, write the number down before you choose panels, because it drives every calculation that follows.
          </p>
