            4. How Much Solar to Refill Each Size
          </h2>

          <p>
            Divide the battery's capacity by about three, and you have the panel wattage that refills it on a typical sunny day. Divide by 2.5 if you want margin for haze and imperfect aim, which most people should. It is a deliberately simple rule, because the inputs to anything more precise, such as exact panel temperature and how carefully the panels get re-aimed, are guesses anyway. The table below applies that rule to the battery sizes in this guide and names the stations that fall in each class.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Battery size</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Panel watts for a full refill</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Panel watts for half a refill</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Example stations</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">~290Wh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">100-120W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">50-60W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">River 3 Plus</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
