                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,300-1,650W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">650-820W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">SOLIX F3800, Delta Pro 3</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Two practical caveats apply. First, a full refill from empty is not always the goal. If you only drain a 1kWh station to about half each night, 200W of panels keeps pace, and the extra 200W in the full-refill column is insurance rather than necessity. Size the array to what you actually use per day, not to the battery label. A household that runs a fridge and a router needs a different array from one that also runs a window air conditioner in the afternoon, even if both own the same station.
          </p>

          <p>
            Second, the station's solar input limit caps the useful array size. Panel wattage above that limit is not lost entirely, because it helps on hazy days, early in the morning and late in the afternoon, when the panels are producing well below their rating. But at noon on a clear day the charge controller will clip the extra. A modest oversize of 10-30 percent is common and sensible, especially with folding panels that rarely see full output; doubling the limit is wasted money that would be better spent on a second battery or a better mount.
          </p>

          <p>
            The table also shows why the big systems are a different purchase. Refilling a 4kWh pack takes 1,300W or more of panel, which means four rigid residential panels or eight or more folding ones. That is a weekend project with a ground rack and cable runs, not something you unfold from the trunk. The 1kWh class, by contrast, refills from two folding panels that one person can set out and put away, which is the main reason it suits most households better than the bigger, heavier units.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are still choosing a station rather than panels, run the same arithmetic the other way. Work out the panel space you realistically have, whether a sunny corner of the yard, a balcony rail or an RV roof, multiply it by the harvest figure for your worst month, and buy a battery that matches what that space can put back. For a camping setup where you want the full picture on capacity, weight and ports, our <a href="/best-portable-power-station-for-camping/">camping power station guide</a> goes deeper on the small end of the range.
          </p>
