        <div id="input-limits" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. MPPT Input Limits and Voltage Windows
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every station on this list uses an MPPT (maximum power point tracking) charge controller. It continually adjusts the load on the panels to pull the most power from them as sunlight changes, which is noticeably more efficient than the older PWM controllers found on cheap kits. But an MPPT input has three limits printed on its spec sheet, and all three matter. Most buyers only look at the wattage figure and then wonder why a third panel made no difference, or why the station refuses to charge at dawn.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Maximum input power</h3>

          <p>
            This is the most the controller will turn into charge, in watts. Panels beyond it are clipped at midday. It is also the number that decides how fast the battery can refill from solar alone: a 1kWh station with a 500W input cannot fill in under about two and a half hours of perfect sun no matter how much glass you connect. On the bigger units the figure is much higher, which is part of what you pay for, but it is still a ceiling to design around rather than a target to exceed by a wide margin.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Voltage window and maximum current</h3>

          <p>
            The voltage window has a minimum below which the controller will not start charging, and a maximum that must never be exceeded. Exceeding the maximum, even briefly, can damage the controller, and manufacturers generally treat over-voltage damage as misuse rather than a warranty fault. The current limit matters when you wire panels in parallel, because parallel wiring adds their currents. If the total exceeds the input's current rating, the controller limits it and the extra panel adds little. Check all three numbers for every panel combination you plan, not just the first one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Large stations such as the Delta Pro 3 and SOLIX F3800 split their solar input across two separate ports, each with its own window. That lets you build two smaller strings rather than one long one, which is easier to keep within voltage limits and less vulnerable to shade. Read each port's limits individually; they are not always identical, and on some models one port is designed for high-voltage residential panels while the other suits lower-voltage folding ones. The manual, not the marketing page, is the place to find the exact figures.
          </p>

          <p>
            A useful habit is to write the three limits on a piece of tape on the station itself, next to the solar port. When you add a panel two years from now, borrow one from a neighbour during a long outage, or let a teenager set up the camping kit, the numbers are right there. It sounds fussy, but over-voltage is the one solar mistake that can permanently damage an otherwise healthy station, and it usually happens when someone improvises with panels they did not buy for that unit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="wiring" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Series, Parallel and Cold-Weather Voltage
          </h2>

          <p>
            Once you have more than one panel, you have to decide how to connect them. In series, positive goes to negative and the voltages add while the current stays the same. In parallel, using a branch connector, positives join positives and the currents add while the voltage stays the same. Either way the total wattage is the same; what changes is how that power reaches the charge controller, and that difference decides whether the system works well at dawn, under partial shade and on a cold morning.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm"></th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Series</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Parallel</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">What adds up</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Voltage</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Current</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Main risk</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Exceeding the max input voltage, especially in cold</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Exceeding the max input current</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Long cable runs</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Better: higher voltage, lower losses</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Worse: higher current needs thicker cable</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Low light</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Reaches the controller's start voltage sooner</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">May sit below start voltage at dawn and dusk</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Partial shade</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">One shaded panel drags the string down</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Shaded panel affects mainly itself</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Mixed panels</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Panels should share the same current</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Panels should share the same voltage</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            For most portable setups the rule is simple: use series when the combined voltage stays comfortably inside the window, including cold-weather headroom; otherwise use parallel, or a mix of both. Large stations with two inputs often work best with one series string per input. And avoid mixing very different panels in the same string, because the weakest panel sets the pace for the rest. A new 200W panel wired in series with an old 100W panel will often deliver little more than the old panel alone.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Voc rises in the cold</h3>

          <p>
            The voltage number that matters for safety is Voc, open-circuit voltage: what a panel produces with nothing connected. It is always higher than the voltage under load, and it is printed on the panel's label. Crucially, Voc rises as the panel gets colder. Silicon panels typically gain somewhere around 0.3 percent of their voltage for every degree Celsius below the 25-degree (77F) test temperature. That is why a string that is comfortably legal in July can be over the limit on a bright January morning, with nothing else about the system changing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Here is an illustrative example with round numbers. Suppose a station's input accepts up to 60V, and you have panels rated at 22V Voc. Two in series gives 44V, which looks fine; three gives 66V, which is already over. Now consider a clear January morning at -10C (14F), 35 degrees below the test temperature. At 0.3 percent per degree, Voc climbs roughly 10 percent, so the two-panel string reads about 48-49V and is still inside the window. But panels rated at 27V Voc would read 54V on paper and close to 60V on that morning, right at the limit with no margin at all.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">LEAVE HEADROOM</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Keep a series string's total Voc 15-20 percent below the station's maximum input voltage.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Do this if you will use the system in freezing weather. Cold, bright mornings after a snowfall, when the panels are cold and the light is reflected, are exactly when voltage peaks. On the battery side, LFP cells should not be charged below freezing, so keep the station indoors and run the panel cable through a door seal or window gap.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="panels" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Portable vs Rigid Panels, and Where to Aim Them
          </h2>

          <p>
            The panel type you choose should follow what the system is for, not what the bundle includes. Folding panels are the right tool when the system moves: they pack flat, set up in minutes and plug straight into the station with the right adapter. Their drawbacks are price per watt, fragility in wind, and the fact that someone has to set them out and re-aim them. Rigid residential panels cost far less per watt, survive hail and snow, and can stay aimed permanently on a simple ground rack.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm"></th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Folding portable panels</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Rigid residential panels</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Typical size</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">100-400W per panel</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Commonly 350-450W per panel</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cost per watt</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Much lower</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Setup</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Unfold, aim, plug in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Needs a ground mount, rack or roof</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Wind and weather</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Must be weighted or put away</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Built to stay out for decades</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Voltage</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Usually lower, simple to wire</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Higher Voc; check series limits carefully</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Best for</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Camping, RVs, occasional outages</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Home backup, daily cycling</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            For a 4kWh home-backup station, a few rigid panels on a ground mount near the house will almost always outperform a pile of folding ones for the same money. The catch with rigid panels is voltage. A full-size residential panel has a much higher Voc than a folding one, so two in series may already be near a mid-size station's input limit. Large stations with high-voltage inputs are designed for this; carry-size stations often are not. Check the numbers before you buy, using the headroom rule from the previous section.
          </p>

          <p>
            Placement matters as much as panel type. Face the panels south in the Northern Hemisphere and avoid anything that throws shadow between mid-morning and mid-afternoon, because that window is where most of the day's energy arrives. Tilt the panel from horizontal by roughly your latitude, then add about 15 degrees in winter when the sun is low and subtract about 15 degrees in summer when it is high. Folding panels with built-in kickstands usually offer only a few fixed angles, so pick the closest one; lying a panel flat on the ground can cost a large share of its winter output.
          </p>

          <p>
            Treat shade as the enemy. A shadow across even a small part of one panel can cut its output sharply, because the cells in each section are wired in series. Bypass diodes limit the damage, but a branch shadow, a vent pipe or a fence line across the bottom row still costs more than its size suggests. If one panel in a series string will be shaded part of the day, consider wiring it in parallel, or moving it. With portable panels at a campsite, turning them to follow the sun two or three times a day noticeably increases harvest.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, keep panels cool and clean. Panels lose output as they heat up, so leave an air gap behind folding panels rather than laying them on hot pavement or a dark car roof, and wipe off dust, pollen and bird droppings with water and a soft cloth. None of these habits is dramatic on its own, but over a week-long outage the difference between a carefully aimed, clean, ventilated panel and one propped flat in the dirt can be a full battery's worth of energy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="bundles" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Bundle or Buy Separately?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every major brand sells its power stations as solar generator bundles with its own panels. The appeal is real: matched connectors, a panel that is guaranteed to sit inside the station's input window, one warranty contact, and sometimes a discount over buying the parts individually. For someone who does not want to think about Voc, MC4 adapters or current limits, a matched bundle removes every way of getting the wiring wrong, and that peace of mind is worth something.
          </p>

          <p>
            The drawbacks are just as real. Bundles are often sized for a photo rather than a refill, with a single panel paired to a battery that needs two or three. Brand-name folding panels also tend to cost more per watt than comparable third-party panels. And for home backup, a bundle of folding panels is usually the wrong kind of panel altogether, because the system will sit in one place for years and would be better served by rigid panels on a permanent mount.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our rule of thumb: buy a bundle for camping and RV systems where folding panels are what you want anyway, and check that the panel wattage roughly matches the battery using the refill table above. Buy separately for home backup, pairing the station with rigid panels, a ground mount and the right adapter cables, all checked against its input voltage, current and wattage. If you are deciding between the two biggest bundle brands, our <a href="/ecoflow-vs-jackery/">EcoFlow vs Jackery comparison</a> breaks down how their panels, inputs and ecosystems differ.
          </p>

          <p>
            When you do buy separately, the parts list is short. You need panels whose combined Voc and current fit the station, an adapter from the panels' MC4 connectors to the station's solar port, an extension cable long enough to reach a sunny spot while the station stays indoors, and a mount or kickstand. A cheap inline fuse or breaker on longer runs is good practice. Buy the adapter from the station maker or confirm its polarity with a meter before connecting, because a reversed adapter is a surprisingly common way to damage a new unit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whichever route you take, confirm what is in the box on the listing you actually buy from. Bundles are reshuffled constantly, the same product name can ship with one panel or two depending on the retailer, and panel wattages change between generations. The station's specification sheet, not the bundle photo, is the document that tells you whether the panels will work together, and it takes five minutes to check against the numbers in this guide.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="home-backup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Home-Backup Solar Sizing: Worked Examples
          </h2>

          <p>
            The process is the same for every house. Add up the energy your essential loads use in a day, divide by 0.85 for inverter losses, then size the battery to cover the night and the array to put the day's use back. The runtime rule from our main guide still applies: hours equals Wh x 0.85 divided by watts. The examples below use round numbers and typical appliance draws rather than measurements from any particular home, so treat them as templates to fill in with your own appliance labels or a plug-in watt meter.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Example 1: Apartment essentials on a 1kWh station</h3>

          <p>
            Refrigerator at about 1.2kWh a day, router and modem at 20W (about 480Wh), LED lamps (150Wh) and phone and laptop charging (100Wh). That is about 1,930Wh a day, or roughly 2,270Wh drawn from the battery once inverter losses are included. A 1kWh station alone lasts around ten to eleven hours. With 400W of folding panels on a balcony putting back about 1.0-1.4kWh on a clear day, you can cover the daytime load as it happens and still put some charge back. Running the fridge in cycles rather than continuously closes the rest of the gap, which stretches a short-outage plan to several days in good weather.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Example 2: Suburban house, essentials on a 4kWh system</h3>

          <p>
            Refrigerator (1.5kWh), chest freezer (0.8kWh), internet (0.5kWh), lights (0.3kWh), charging (0.1kWh), and a gas furnace blower running about 150W for four hours a day in winter (0.6kWh). Total about 3.8kWh a day, or about 4.5kWh from the battery. A Delta Pro 3 or SOLIX F3800 covers most of a full day on its own. To keep up indefinitely, the array must put back about 4.5kWh a day: at roughly 3Wh per rated watt, that is about 1,500W of panels in summer and closer to 2,000W or more for the shorter days of winter. Four or five rigid panels on a ground mount, split into two strings across the station's two inputs, is the realistic shape of this system. Our <a href="/best-portable-power-station-for-home-backup/">home-backup power station guide</a> covers the transfer-switch side in more depth.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Example 3: Rural house with a 240V well pump</h3>

          <p>
            Add a 240V well pump to Example 2. Its daily energy may be modest if water use is careful, but its starting surge is large, which is why the 6,000W, 120/240V SOLIX F3800 or the Delta Pro 3's 240V output matter here. The solar sizing barely changes; the inverter choice does. Schedule laundry and showers for the sunny middle of the day, when panels are carrying part of the load directly, and the battery saves its energy for the night. Check the pump's nameplate starting current against the station's surge rating before you commit.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Example 4: Medical device backup</h3>

          <p>
            A CPAP at 40W for eight hours a night uses about 320Wh, or about 375Wh from the battery. A 1kWh station covers two to three nights on its own, and even a single 200W panel replaces one night's use on most clear days. For anyone who depends on a device, this is the cheapest and most reliable solar generator setup there is. Confirm the station's UPS switchover time against the device maker's guidance, and keep a few charged power banks from our <a href="/best-portable-chargers-for-power-outages/">portable chargers for power outages</a> list for phones so the station's energy goes to the device.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Scenario</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Daily use (with losses)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Battery</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Panels to break even</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">CPAP only</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~375Wh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~1kWh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~150-200W</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Apartment essentials</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~2.3kWh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-2kWh</td>
