        <div id="choosing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Which Pairing to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            For camping, van life and day trips, the EcoFlow River 3 Plus with one 100-200W folding panel is the answer. It is light, simple, and the panel genuinely keeps up with what a campsite uses: a cooler, lights, phones and maybe a CPAP. Nothing else on this list is as easy to carry from the car to a pitch, and the optional extra battery means it can grow a little for longer trips without becoming a two-person lift.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most households that want outage backup plus the occasional camping trip, choose one of the 1kWh stations, the EcoFlow Delta 3 Plus, Jackery Explorer 1000 v2 or Bluetti Elite 100 V2, with 300-400W of panels. Buy the Jackery as a two-panel bundle rather than a single-panel one, buy the Bluetti with third-party panels checked against its input window, and choose the EcoFlow if you think you might add a second battery later. All three run a refrigerator and refill from two folding panels on a sunny day.
          </p>

          <p>
            For RVs and longer outages in sunny regions, the Jackery Explorer 2000 v2 with 400-600W of folding panels is the step up. It roughly doubles the overnight reserve without crossing into the wheeled, 100-pound class, and two or three folding panels on an RV roof rack or picnic table keep it cycling through a sunny week. Watch the input limit before you add the third panel.
          </p>

          <p>
            For whole-home essentials and multi-day outages, the EcoFlow Delta Pro 3 or Anker SOLIX F3800 with 1,200-2,000W of rigid panels on a ground mount is the real solar generator, connected to the house through a transfer switch by a licensed electrician. Choose the F3800 if a 240V well pump or heavy motor loads are on the list, and the Delta Pro 3 if you value EcoFlow's home-integration hardware and a slightly larger base battery. Either way, budget for the array and the installation, not only the station.
          </p>

          <p>
            And if all you need is to keep phones and a few lights alive through an overnight outage, you may not need a solar generator at all. A good power bank or a small station charged from the wall is enough, and our <a href="/best-portable-chargers-for-power-outages/">power-outage charger guide</a> covers those. When you want the broader view of capacity classes, chemistry and runtime beyond the solar side, the <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">full portable power station ranking</a> is the place to start.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the battery and treating panels as an afterthought</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 4kWh station with one 200W folding panel is a big battery you refill from the wall. Decide how much energy you need back each day, size the array to that using the divide-by-three rule, and only then choose the station whose input limits can accept it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Planning around the panel's label wattage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Portable panels typically deliver 60-75 percent of their rating in good sun, and far less on cloudy or winter days. Plan with roughly 2.5-3.5Wh per rated panel watt per day, then check the sun hours for your worst outage month rather than a flattering summer average.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Exceeding the input voltage on a cold morning</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Panel Voc rises about 0.3 percent per degree Celsius below 25C. A series string that is fine in July can exceed the station's limit on a bright January morning. Keep the total 15-20 percent below the maximum input voltage, and remember over-voltage damage is usually treated as misuse.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the bundle to be correctly sized</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many bundles pair one panel with a battery that needs two or three to refill in a day. Before buying, divide the battery's watt-hours by three and compare it with the panel wattage in the box. If the panel falls well short, add a second or buy the parts separately.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the station outside in freezing weather</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                LFP cells should not be charged below freezing, and most stations block charging when too cold. Keep the station indoors or in an insulated space and run the panel cable through a door seal or window gap. The panels can live outside; the battery should not in winter.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a solar generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A solar generator is a portable power station paired with solar panels. The station contains a lithium battery, an inverter that produces household AC power, and an MPPT charge controller that turns panel output into stored energy. Nothing is burned, so it is silent and safe indoors while the panels sit outside. The term is mostly marketing; what makes one good is how well battery size, input limits and panel wattage are matched.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many solar panels do I need for a solar generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Divide the battery capacity in watt-hours by about three to get the panel wattage that refills it on a typical sunny day, or by 2.5 for margin. A 286Wh camping unit needs about 100W, a 1kWh station about 350-400W, a 2kWh station about 700-800W, and a 4kWh system about 1,300-1,650W. Use less if you only drain part of the battery, and always stay inside the station's input limits.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does it take to charge a solar generator with solar panels?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on panel wattage, weather and battery size. Portable panels deliver roughly 60-75 percent of their rating in good sun, and a clear day offers about four to five peak sun hours. A 200W panel puts back about 500-700Wh a day, enough to refill a 286Wh unit by midday or half of a 1kWh station. With 400W, a 1kWh station refills in one sunny day.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use any solar panel with my power station?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually, if three things fit. The panel's open-circuit voltage, or the total for a series string, must stay below the station's maximum input voltage with cold-weather headroom. The current, or the total for panels in parallel, must not exceed the input rating. And you need the right connector or adapter. Wattage above the input limit will not harm the station but will be clipped at midday.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I wire my panels in series or parallel?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Series adds voltage, parallel adds current, and total wattage is the same. Series is usually better when it fits the voltage window, because it reaches the controller's start threshold earlier and loses less power over long cables. Parallel is safer when series would exceed the maximum voltage, and it copes better with partial shade. Large stations with two inputs often work best with one series string on each.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a solar generator power a house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A large one can power a house's essential circuits, and with enough panels it can keep doing so through a long outage. Systems such as the EcoFlow Delta Pro 3 and Anker SOLIX F3800 store roughly 4kWh and are rated for 4,000-6,000W at 120/240V. Fed by 1,500-2,000W of rigid panels through a transfer switch, they can run a fridge, freezer, furnace blower, lights and internet. Central air and electric heat are beyond them.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a solar generator better than a gas generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Each wins in different situations. A solar generator is silent, produces no exhaust, runs indoors, needs no fuel storage and costs almost nothing to run once bought, which suits apartments, medical devices, camping and short outages. A gas generator delivers more power for longer at a lower upfront cost and keeps going through a week of storms. Many prepared households use both, with the gas unit refilling the battery during cloud.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Related Guides -->
